        <div id="membership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What the Echelon Membership Costs, and What the Treadmill Does Without It</h2>
<p>
            Echelon's paid tier is called Premier. On 20 October 2026 Echelon's product pages
            offered it at $39.99 for 30 days, $399.99 for a year or $699.99 for two years, with free
            shipping on the treadmill when you take an annual or two-year plan. A cheaper FitPass
            tier at $11.99 a month covers off-equipment workouts such as yoga and strength, and
            Echelon's own fine print says equipment-based classes are not included in it. These are
            Echelon's prices on that day and they change; check the checkout page before you commit.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Premier adds</h3>
          <p>
            Echelon lists live and on-demand classes across its equipment, up to five member
            profiles per account, scenic and gamified workouts, AI class recommendations, a
            workout builder, entertainment streaming on screened machines and — the one buyers
            tend to overlook — the extended warranty covered in section 7. Over five years, the
            annual plan alone comes to about $2,000 at today's price, which is more than the
            Stride-6 itself.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What still works without it</h3>
          <p>
            Echelon's product and membership pages do not say, which is itself worth noting.
            Independent reviewers describe it this way. TreadmillReviews.com says you need a free
            Echelon account to train in manual mode on the Stride-6; Run to the Finish says of the
            Stride-8s that "an account is needed to use the treadmill, a membership is only needed
            for class access"; BarBend says the treadmills run in manual mode without a membership
            but with limited function. In other words, the belt, speed and incline should work, and
            the classes, programmes and much of the screen's value should not. An earlier
            independent review also reported that Echelon required at least one month of Premier with
            a purchase; its pages now present the membership as an add-on and advertise a free
            trial, which is one more reason to confirm the current terms in writing.
          </p>
          <p>
            One further report deserves weight. Independent coverage of Echelon's bikes says a
            software update made them work only while connected to Echelon's servers. We found no
            equivalent report for the treadmills, but a machine that needs an account to start is
            a machine whose basic use depends on the company's software. If that matters to you, ask
            Echelon before buying whether the Stride you are considering runs with no internet
            connection, and what happens to manual mode if your account or the app is unavailable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="screen-or-tablet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Built-In Screen or Your Own Tablet?</h2>
<p>
            This is the choice between the Stride-6 and the Stride-6s, and on Echelon's prices on
            the day it was worth $100. The two share the frame, the deck, the auto-fold, the incline
            and the weight rating. The Stride-6s adds a 10-inch touchscreen and a slightly larger
            motor rating — 3.0 CHP against the Stride-6's "DC 2.5" — and the Stride-6 has an LED
            readout with a large device holder instead.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The case for your own tablet</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>It may well be bigger.</strong> Many current tablets measure 10 inches or more, so a tablet on the Stride-6 can match or beat the screen built into the Stride-6s.</li>
            <li><strong>It is not tied to one app.</strong> A tablet runs whatever you like — Echelon's app, a video service, another training app — where Echelon's screens are built around Echelon's own content.</li>
            <li><strong>It can be replaced.</strong> A built-in screen ages with the treadmill and its software support; a tablet can be upgraded on its own, and it is still yours if you sell the treadmill.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The case for the built-in screen</h3>
          <p>
            It is always there, always charged and always mounted at the right angle, and it gives
            you the extra motor rating for $100. If the plan is to follow Echelon classes most days,
            the Stride-6s is less fiddly. The Stride-8s's 22-inch screen swivels for floor workouts,
            but costs $450 more again and gives up the auto-fold.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Zwift and other apps</h3>
          <p>
            Echelon does not name Zwift, Kinomap or the Bluetooth FTMS fitness-machine standard on
            its Stride product pages, and owners on Zwift's forum report that the original Stride
            did not pair with Zwift directly. If open-app running is the plan, assume you will need
            a <a href="/treadmill-foot-pod-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>foot pod</a>, or buy a treadmill whose maker names the app in its listing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fold-and-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Auto-Fold, Footprint and Space</h2>
<p>
            The auto-fold is the reason Echelon treadmills are worth a page of their own. Most <a href="/folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>folding treadmills</a> tilt the deck up so that it stands beside the console, which saves
            floor space but leaves a tall object in the room. On the Stride-6 and Stride-6s, a button
            collapses the console down onto the deck so that the whole machine lies flat, and
            transport wheels let you either roll it under furniture or stand it on end against a
            wall.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The figures</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Assembled:</strong> Echelon lists the Stride-6 and Stride-6s at 64.25 inches long, 32 wide and 57 high; one independent review gives 64.8 by 32.4 by 57 inches.</li>
            <li><strong>Folded height:</strong> Echelon does not publish one. BarBend says they fold flat to about 10 inches; another independent review gives about 8.5 inches at the deck and about 12 at the console. Measure the gap under any furniture against the highest figure.</li>
            <li><strong>Weight:</strong> 182.35 lb on Echelon's listing and an independent review. That is light for a 60-inch deck, but still a two-person lift on stairs.</li>
            <li><strong>Step-up height:</strong> 7 inches on Echelon's listing, which we could not confirm elsewhere.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The Stride-8s is larger, at 67 by 33.1 by 63 inches assembled, and lies flat only after
            you remove two screws, so in practice it stays put. The Stride 50 RCX-22 folds the
            conventional way, to 43.6 by 34.9 by 41.9 inches on Echelon's figures.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the fold costs</h3>
          <p>
            A machine light enough to fold itself is lighter in other ways too: a 20.5-inch deck
            rather than the 22 inches recommended for bigger runners, a 300 lb rating rather than the
            350 to 400 lb of heavier frames, and a motor in the 2.5 to 3.0 CHP range. For walking and
            moderate running that is a fair trade; for heavy daily running it is not. Our <a href="/best-low-profile-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">low-profile treadmill roundup</a> sets the Stride-6s against other machines that store low.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Echelon Warranty, and Why It Depends on the Membership</h2>
<p>
            Echelon's published warranty terms give a base limited warranty of one year on all
            equipment, five years on treadmill motors and ten years on frames, which Echelon defines
            as the non-moving skeleton and welded metal structure. Independent reviews of the Stride
            range quote the same terms.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Premier extension</h3>
          <p>
            Active Premier members get four additional years of cover on top of the one-year base,
            for five years in total. Echelon's product pages call this a "Premier 4-Year Warranty
            Guarantee" and its membership page a "5-Year Warranty Guarantee"; both describe the same
            thing, counted differently. Three conditions in Echelon's terms matter more than the
            headline:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>It must stay active.</strong> The terms say you must be and continue to be an active Premier member, and past members are not eligible. Let the membership lapse and the cover falls back to the base terms.</li>
            <li><strong>It does not extend the motor or frame.</strong> The extension adds nothing to the five-year motor or ten-year frame terms; what it lengthens is the one-year cover on everything else.</li>
            <li><strong>It is not transferable.</strong> Echelon's warranty extends only to the original purchaser, which matters if you buy or sell second-hand.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Shipping and claims</h3>
          <p>
            Echelon's terms say it pays shipping on repaired or replacement products for the first
            12 months and charges a fee after that, except for frame claims, and that no warranty
            service is provided without a return authorisation number. Labour is not broken out as
            a separate term. Put plainly, from the second year a non-member is paying for most
            repairs that are not the motor or the frame. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> explains how to read terms like these, and why a five-year motor term is short next to
            the lifetime motor cover several rivals offer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="echelon-vs-peloton-ifit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Echelon vs Peloton Tread vs NordicTrack and ProForm</h2>
<p>
            All three are platform treadmills: the maker sells the hardware and a membership, and
            the screen is built to sell the membership. The differences are price, fold and how much
            each machine does on its own.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Echelon vs Peloton</h3>
          <p>
            Peloton replaced its treadmill line on 1 October 2026 with the <a href="/peloton-tread/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Tread Flex</a> from $2,195,
            the Tread Vision from $3,495 and the Tread+ Vision from $6,695, on its own site on 20
            October. The Tread Flex is Peloton's first folding treadmill, with a 21.5-inch screen,
            0 to 12.5 mph, 0 to 12.5% incline, a 59 by 20-inch belt and a 300 lb rating; it folds to
            37.8 inches deep rather than lying flat. Peloton says its All-Access Membership is
            required for its content and features.
          </p>
          <p>
            Against that, an Echelon Stride-6s is about $400 cheaper than a Tread Flex, has a
            slightly longer and wider deck, and stores lower, but has a far smaller screen. The
            Stride-8s is the closer match on screen size, at about $55 more than the Tread Flex on
            the day's prices, and it does not auto-fold either.
            The Peloton line is too new for long-term independent coverage of reliability. If you
            have already decided on Peloton's instructors, buy the Peloton; the classes are the
            product. Our <a href="/best-peloton-alternatives/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton alternatives guide</a> covers the rest of that decision.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Echelon vs NordicTrack and ProForm</h3>
          <p>
            NordicTrack and ProForm, both iFIT brands, are the more conventional comparison. Their
            folding models tilt up rather than lie flat, and most are heavier, with wider decks and
            higher weight ratings in the upper models. NordicTrack says in writing that manual mode
            is always available without an iFIT membership, which is a clearer statement than any
            we found from Echelon, and iFIT workouts can drive the treadmill's speed and incline
            automatically. Our <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT guide</a> sets out what that membership is worth.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to decide</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Space first.</strong> If the treadmill must vanish under a bed or behind a sofa, only the Stride-6 and Stride-6s do that at full length.</li>
            <li><strong>Classes second.</strong> Choose the platform whose classes you will actually follow; try each app's free trial on a phone before buying hardware.</li>
            <li><strong>Five-year cost third.</strong> Add five years of membership to the hardware price, and remember that Echelon's longer warranty only exists while you pay.</li>
            <li><strong>No platform at all.</strong> If none of the classes appeal, a plain-console treadmill will do more for the money.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Buying New, Open-Box or Used</h2>
<p>
            Echelon sells direct through echelonfit.com, and its treadmills appear at large
            retailers such as Best Buy. On the day we checked Echelon advertised a 60-day home trial,
            0% APR financing and a free Premier trial with every treadmill. Read the return terms
            before you rely on the trial: ask who pays return freight on a 182 lb machine, whether
            there is a restocking fee, and whether the membership you start at checkout renews
            automatically. Retailers' own return windows can differ from Echelon's.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Certified open-box</h3>
          <p>
            Echelon's open-box units were priced several hundred dollars below new on the day we
            checked. Ask in writing whether an open-box unit carries the same warranty as a new one
            and whether it is the current revision of the model. The saving is worthwhile only if
            the warranty is.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Used</h3>
          <p>
            A used Echelon carries two particular risks. The warranty does not transfer to a second
            owner, and the machine may need an Echelon account to run, so ask the seller to remove
            it from their account and test that it starts on yours before you pay. Run the auto-fold
            several times, step through the full incline range and listen to the motor at your real
            speed. Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a> covers the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy an Echelon Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy one if:</strong> you live in a small space and need a full 60-inch deck
            that folds flat out of sight; you mainly walk or run at moderate pace; you weigh well
            under 300 lb; and you want guided classes and will keep a Premier membership, which also
            keeps the longer warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Choose the Stride-6</strong> if you already own a good tablet and want the
            lowest price on the auto-fold. <strong>Choose the Stride-6s</strong> if you want the
            screen built in and the slightly larger motor rating for $100 more. <strong>Choose the
            Stride-8s</strong> only if a 22-inch screen matters more than the fold, and compare it with
            a Peloton Tread Flex before you do.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere if:</strong> you run hard most days, are a tall or heavy runner
            who needs a 22-inch deck or a rating above 300 lb, want to use <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Zwift</a> or other open apps
            directly, or do not want an ongoing subscription. In those cases the warranty and the
            frame of a plain-console machine are worth more than the fold.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
            This guide is about equipment, not health. If you are buying a treadmill on a doctor's or
            physical therapist's advice — for rehabilitation, a joint problem or a heart condition —
            ask them what speed, incline and deck size suit you before choosing a model; nothing
            here is medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying an Echelon Treadmill</h2>
          <p>An Echelon is a good answer to a specific problem. These are the ways buyers end up with the wrong one, or the right one on the wrong terms.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Counting the five-year warranty without the membership</h3>
              <p>Echelon’s five-year cover exists only while Premier stays active; let it lapse and most parts fall back to one year. Before buying, decide honestly whether you will keep paying. If not, judge the machine on its one-year, five-year motor and ten-year frame terms, and compare those with rivals’ base terms rather than Echelon’s headline.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying for the fold without measuring the gap</h3>
              <p>Echelon publishes no folded height, and independent figures range from about 8.5 inches at the deck to about 12 at the console. Buyers who plan to slide the treadmill under a bed or sofa and measure against the lower figure find it does not fit. Measure the clearance and plan for the highest figure, plus the transport wheels.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying for a 10-inch screen when your tablet is bigger</h3>
              <p>The Stride-6s’s main addition over the Stride-6 is a 10-inch touchscreen, and many tablets are as large or larger. If you already own one, the Stride-6 with your tablet in its device holder gives as big a picture or bigger and works with other apps. Pay the extra only if you want the screen permanently mounted or value the larger motor rating.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Choosing an Echelon for Zwift or heavy running</h3>
              <p>Echelon does not name Zwift or Bluetooth FTMS on its Stride pages, and owners report the original Stride would not pair directly. The deck is 20 to 20.5 inches wide and the rating 300 lb. If you plan open-app runs or hard daily training, buy a machine whose maker names the app and lists a wider deck, a higher rating and longer motor cover.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">An Echelon treadmill is the right buy for a small space and a
          class-led plan: the Stride-6s gives a full 60-inch deck that folds itself flat, with a
          built-in screen, for $1,799.99 on the day we checked.</strong> The Stride-6 does the same
          for $100 less if you bring your own tablet, and the Stride-8s trades the fold for a 22-inch
          screen.
          </p>
          <p>
            Go in knowing the terms: a 300 lb rating, a modest motor, a free account needed even for
          manual running according to independent reviews, and a warranty that is five years only
          while Premier stays active. If you want none of the classes, a plain-console machine will
          serve you better — see our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">no-subscription treadmill guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

