        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Are Echelon treadmills good?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For the right buyer, yes. The Stride-6 and Stride-6s pair a full 20.5 by 60-inch deck with an auto-fold that lays the machine flat, which few full-size treadmills do. The trade-offs are a 300 lb rating, a modest motor, a one-year base warranty on most parts and a design built around Echelon’s paid membership. We have not tested them.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you use an Echelon treadmill without a membership?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Independent reviews say yes, in a manual mode, but that you need at least a free Echelon account to run it. Classes, programmes and most of the screen’s features need Premier, which Echelon offered at $39.99 for 30 days or $399.99 a year on 20 October 2026. Echelon’s own pages do not spell this out, so confirm before buying.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between the Echelon Stride-6 and Stride-6s?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The Stride-6s adds a 10-inch touchscreen and a 3.0 CHP motor rating; the Stride-6 has an LED console, a large device holder for your own tablet and a motor Echelon lists as DC 2.5. They share the 20.5 by 60-inch deck, auto-fold, 12 incline levels and 300 lb rating. Echelon priced them $100 apart on 20 October 2026.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What warranty do Echelon treadmills have?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Echelon’s published terms give one year on all equipment, five years on the treadmill motor and ten years on the frame. Active Premier members get four extra years of cover, for five in total, but the extension lapses if the membership does, does not lengthen the motor or frame terms, and does not transfer to a second owner.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How flat does an Echelon treadmill fold?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The Stride-6 and Stride-6s fold themselves flat at the touch of a button and can also stand on end. Echelon does not publish a folded height; BarBend gives about 10 inches and another review about 8.5 inches at the deck and 12 at the console. The Stride-8s lies flat only after removing two screws.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is Echelon better than Peloton for a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is cheaper and stores lower. The Stride-6s was about $400 less than Peloton’s new folding Tread Flex on 20 October 2026 and lies flat, while the Tread Flex folds to 37.8 inches deep and has a 21.5-inch screen. Both rely on a paid membership. Choose on whose classes you will actually use.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Have Echelon treadmills been recalled?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission’s recall database, including every recall since 2014, on 20 October 2026 and found no recall of any Echelon or FitNation product. Peloton recalled its original Tread and Tread+ in 2021; those models have since been replaced.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
